{"page": 48, "text": "During the nine months ended September 30, 2026, our Ambulatory Care segment paid an aggregate of $211 million to acquire controlling ownership interests in 13 ASCs and noncontrolling ownership interests in an additional two ASCs. In the same period, this segment also commenced operations at four de novo ASCs and ceased operations at 17 ASCs and one surgical hospital. Consolidated Gains and Losses on Sales, Consolidation and Deconsolidation of Facilities We recorded gains from the sale, consolidation and deconsolidation of facilities totaling $43 million during the nine months ended September 30, 2026. The activity during this period, substantially all of which occurred in our Ambulatory Care segment, primarily included gains totaling $23 million from the consolidation of four ASCs and gains of $16 million related to the closure or sale of ASCs. We recorded net gains from the sale, consolidation and deconsolidation of facilities totaling $4 million during the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The activity during this period primarily included a gain of $10 million related to post\u2011closing adjustments from our Hospital Operations segment\u2019s 2024 divestiture of five hospitals and certain related operations located in Alabama, gains of $4 million from the sale of facilities by our Ambulatory Care segment and gains of $2 million related to other activity, partially offset by losses of $12 million related to the consolidation of certain facilities by our Ambulatory Care segment. 36 Table of Contents Net Income Available to Noncontrolling Interests The table below presents net income available to noncontrolling interests by segment for the periods indicated:\nThree Months Ended\nSeptember 30, Nine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\n2026 2025 2026 2025\nHospital Operations $ 9 $ 37 $ 27 $ 115\nAmbulatory Care 210 200 615 572\nTotal net income available to noncontrolling interests $ 219 $ 237 $ 642 $ 687\n"}
{"page": 49, "text": "LIQUIDITY AND CAPITAL RESOURCES CASH REQUIREMENTS There have been no material changes to our obligations to make future cash payments under scheduled contractual obligations, such as debt and lease agreements, and under contingent commitments, such as standby letters of credit and minimum revenue guarantees, as disclosed in our Annual Report, except for the matters set forth below and the additional lease and other long-term debt obligations disclosed in Notes 1 and 5, respectively, to our accompanying Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements. Long-Term Debt Interest payments, net of capitalized interest, were $464 million and $499 million in the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively. Other Cash Requirements Capital expenditures were $546 million and $646 million in the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively. We anticipate that our capital expenditures for the year ending December 31, 2026 will total approximately $700 million to $800 million, including $111 million that was accrued as a liability at December 31, 2025. We made income tax payments, net of tax refunds, of $493 million during the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and $329 million during the same period in 2025. The current portion of our income tax payable was $22 million at September 30, 2026, with no corresponding current liability outstanding at December 31, 2025. SOURCES AND USES OF CASH Our liquidity for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 was primarily derived from net cash provided by operating activities, cash on hand and the issuance of long-term debt. Our operating cash flow is impacted by levels of cash collections, as well as levels of implicit price concessions, due to shifts in payer mix and other factors. Our senior secured revolving credit facility (the \u201cCredit Agreement\u201d) provides additional liquidity to manage fluctuations in operating cash caused by these factors. Net cash provided by operating activities was $3.507 billion in the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $2.809 billion in the nine months ended September 30, 2025. Key factors contributing to the change between the 2026 and 2025 periods included the following: \u2022Contract termination payments received of $540 million in the 2026 period; \u2022Interest payments that were $35 million lower in the 2026 period; \u2022A $164 million increase in income tax payments during the 2026 period; and \u2022The timing of working capital items. Net cash used in investing activities decreased by $68 million, or 8%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to the same period in 2025. This decrease was primarily attributable to (1) lower capital expenditures, largely related to the completion of our Florida Coast Medical Center in September 2025, and (2) lower spending on acquisitions of controlling interests in businesses and joint ventures due to the timing of transactions, partially offset by (3) an increase in expenditures for marketable securities and\n"}
{"page": 50, "text": "long-term investments, reflecting the investment of funds held for insurance-related obligations and increased equity investments to support the continued growth of our Ambulatory Care segment. 37 Table of Contents Net cash used in financing activities increased by $931 million, or 47%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to the same period in 2025. This increase was primarily due to Conifer\u2019s redemption of CHI\u2019s minority interest effective January 1, 2026 and an increase in cash used to purchase our common stock during the 2026 period. Financing activities in 2026 also included the issuance of our 2034 Senior Notes, the net proceeds from which, together with cash on hand, were used to finance the redemption of all of our 2027 Senior Secured First Lien Notes and the partial redemption of our 2028 Senior Notes. These transactions reduced our secured debt and refinanced a portion of our near-term maturities with notes due in 2034. For additional information, see Note 5 to the accompanying Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements. We repurchase shares of our common stock from time to time under our share repurchase program, at management\u2019s discretion subject to market conditions and other factors. The program has no expiration date, does not require any minimum amount of repurchases and may be suspended or discontinued at any time. At September 30, 2026, there was $1.875 billion available under the program for future repurchases. DEBT INSTRUMENTS, GUARANTEES AND RELATED COVENANTS At September 30, 2026, we had outstanding senior unsecured notes and senior secured first lien notes with aggregate principal amounts outstanding of $12.662 billion. These notes have fixed interest rates and require semi-annual interest payments in arrears. The principal and any accrued but unpaid interest is due upon the maturity date of the respective notes, which dates are staggered from June 2028 through September 2034. We believe that the fixed-rate structure of these notes helps to reduce the near-term impact of increases in interest rates, and their staggered maturities allow us to retire or refinance our debt over time. Our Credit Agreement is an additional source of liquidity to manage fluctuations in operating cash. At September 30, 2026, we had no cash borrowings outstanding under the Credit Agreement. Based on our eligible accounts receivable, eligible inventory and Medicaid supplemental payments, $1.900 billion was available for borrowing under the Credit Agreement at September 30, 2026. In addition, we have a letter of credit facility that provides for the issuance of standby and documentary letters of credit in an aggregate principal amount of up to $200 million. At September 30, 2026, we had $105 million of standby letters of credit outstanding under this facility. Our Credit Agreement, letter of credit facility, and senior unsecured and senior secured first lien notes contain certain covenants and conditions. We were in compliance\n"}
{"page": 51, "text": "with all such applicable covenants and conditions at September 30, 2026. For additional information regarding our long-term debt, see Note 5 to the accompanying Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements and Note 8 to the Consolidated Financial Statements included in our Annual Report. LIQUIDITY We believe that existing cash and cash equivalents on hand, borrowing availability under our Credit Agreement and anticipated future cash provided by our operating activities are adequate to meet our current cash needs. These sources of liquidity, in combination with any potential future debt incurrence, are adequate to finance planned capital expenditures, payments on the current portion of our long-term debt, payments to current and former joint venture partners, and other presently known operating needs. From time to time, we expect to engage in additional capital markets, bank credit and other financing activities depending on our needs and the financing alternatives available at that time. Long-term liquidity for debt service and other purposes will be dependent on the amount of cash provided by operating activities and, subject to favorable market and other conditions, the successful completion of future borrowings and potential refinancings. Our cash requirements could be materially affected by the use of cash for acquisitions of businesses, repurchases of securities, the exercise of put rights or other exit options by our joint venture partners, and contractual or regulatory commitments to fund capital expenditures in, or intercompany borrowings to, businesses we own. Changes in federal and state statutes, regulations and executive orders, particularly those impacting government healthcare funding, could adversely affect our liquidity. In addition, our liquidity could be adversely affected should there be a deterioration in our results of operations, including our ability to generate sufficient cash from operations, as well as by the various risks and uncertainties discussed herein and the Risk Factors section in Part I of our Annual Report. CRITICAL ACCOUNTING ESTIMATES In preparing our Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements in conformity with accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America, we must use estimates and assumptions that affect the amounts reported in our 38 Table of Contents Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements and accompanying notes. A discussion of our critical accounting estimates is provided in the Critical Accounting Estimates section in Part II of our Annual Report. ITEM 3. QUANTITATIVE AND QUALITATIVE DISCLOSURES ABOUT MARKET RISK The following table presents information about certain of our market-sensitive financial instruments at September 30, 2026. The fair values were determined based on quoted market prices for the same or similar instruments. The average effective interest rates presented are based on the rate in effect at the end of the reporting period. The effects of\n"}
